<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Keeping Our Communities Clean – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Bring the Cleaning Supplies?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Write questions for a classroom research project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can list steps to organize a cleanup (e.g., 'Gather gloves, bags, and bins.').<br>Can make a supplies list for cleanup activities (e.g., paper towels, trash bags).<br>Can write questions for a cleanup research project collaboratively (e.g., 'What day is best for cleaning?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of a dirty classroom/schoolyard and a clean one. Ask students: "What do you see?" "What needs to be done?" Then, instruct students: "Quickly write down 3 words or short phrases that describe what we need to clean or what supplies we need." (e.g., 'trash', 'bags', 'sweep'). This activates prior vocabulary from previous lessons and focuses on immediate writing output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes on the board: "Our classroom is dirty. We need to clean it." Then, models writing a simple question: "What should we do?" or "Who will help us?" The teacher emphasizes how questions help us plan. The teacher also models word stress on imperative commands like 'clean' and 'bring' if students read their ideas aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing sentences or questions today?" (Expected answer: Questions.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write questions to help us plan?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short "Community Cleanup Plan" on the board or a handout, which includes a list of steps and some frequently asked questions (FAQs). For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; border-radius: 5px;">
                    <b>Community Cleanup Plan:</b><br>
                    1. Gather supplies: bags, gloves, tools.<br>
                    2. Organize volunteers into teams.<br>
                    3. Clean the park area.<br>
                    4. Sort recycling and trash.<br><br>
                    <b>FAQs:</b><br>
                    - What day is best for cleaning? (On Saturday.)<br>
                    - Who will bring the cleaning supplies? (Volunteers can bring them.)<br>
                    - Where should we put the trash bags? (We must put them near the gate.)<br>
                    - How can we help our environment? (We should recycle paper and plastic.)<br>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking for and giving information; Describing plans and tasks; Making suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the plan and FAQs silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks gist questions: "What is this text about?" (A cleanup plan). "What do the questions ask about?" (About the cleanup.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and underline all the question words (what, who, where, how) and modal verbs (will, can, must, should) in the FAQs.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing questions using modal verbs (can, should, must), future tense (will), imperatives, and question words (what, how, where). Vocabulary: community, supplies, trash, tasks, bags, recycling, tools, cleanup, volunteers, team, responsibility, clean, bring, pick up, collect, organize, quick, careful, prepared, responsible, organized, needed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides incomplete questions or statements for students to complete or rewrite as questions, focusing on structure. (e.g., "_____ will we clean the area?" -> *When* will we clean the area?; "We ___ use big trash bags." -> We *should* use big trash bags.; "_____ bring gloves for the team?" -> *Who* can bring gloves for the team?). Students write the answers individually on a worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Question Transformation:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives short statements about a cleanup. Students rewrite these into questions or imperative commands, focusing on using different question words, modal verbs, or future tense. (e.g., "The park is dirty." -> "How can we clean the park?"; "We need many helpers." -> "Who will be our helpers?"; "Bring many bags." - Teacher reminds of word stress on 'bring'). Students write their questions in their notebooks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students exchange their written questions from activity 2. They read each other's questions and check for clarity, correct question words, and appropriate modal verbs. They politely suggest improvements (e.g., "Maybe you can use 'where' here?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Writing – "Our Cleanup Research Project Questions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group imagines they are planning a cleanup research project for their classroom or school community to find the best way to keep it clean.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to collaboratively write a list of 5-7 research questions they would ask to gather information for their project. The questions should address key aspects like supplies, volunteers, timing, locations, and tasks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage the use of all learned question words (what, how, where) and modal verbs (can, should, must), as well as future tense (will). (e.g., "What supplies will we need?", "Where should we clean?", "Who can help collect trash?", "Must we sort the recycling?", "How will we organize the teams?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write their list of questions on a large sheet of paper or poster board. This directly addresses the specific objective: "Writing: Write questions for a classroom research project" and the learning outcome "Can write questions for a cleanup research project collaboratively."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> The teacher circulates and observes groups during Stage 4, noting participation, collaboration, and accuracy in question formation. A checklist can be used to track the correct use of question words, modal verbs, and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their list of 5-7 research questions to the class. One student from each group reads out their questions. The teacher provides immediate, gentle feedback focusing on clarity and comprehensibility rather than strict grammatical perfection, reinforcing phonemic awareness by highlighting short and long vowel sounds in key words like '/ɪ/ in 'bring' and /iː/ in 'clean' if they are mispronounced.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (community, supplies, trash, organize, volunteers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of question words (what, how, where).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs (can, should, must) and future tense (will).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and comprehensibility of written questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new question I can write is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing that was difficult today was..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Self-reflection by students and teacher on the lesson's effectiveness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing questions help us plan for a cleanup?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson will help me when I talk about community actions with my group?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students in writing questions for a project? (e.g., group work, visual prompts).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What grammatical structures or vocabulary needed more reinforcement during the writing tasks? Were the instructions clear enough for 6th graders at A2.1 level?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence starters, visual dictionaries) might be needed for the next lesson to support students in consolidating their understanding of planning and responsibility for community actions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did students demonstrate their ability to participate in group discussions on community actions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use the questions they wrote today to summarize and present information about organizing a community cleanup project, potentially creating a final "Cleanup Action Plan" based on the answers to their research questions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>